Chapter 3: Network Topologies
What is a Network Topology?

A network topology is the arrangement or layout of devices (computers, switches, routers, etc.) and the connections between them.

Think of it as the road map of a network.

Real-Life Analogy

Imagine a city:

Roads connect houses.
Different road designs affect traffic.
If one road is blocked, some layouts handle it better than others.

A computer network works the same way. The topology determines how devices communicate and how failures affect the network.

Why Do We Need Different Topologies?

Different organizations have different requirements.

For example:

A small office may use a Star Topology because it's easy to manage.
A telecom provider may use a Mesh Topology for high reliability.
An old Ethernet network might have used a Bus Topology.

The choice depends on:

Cost
Reliability
Performance
Ease of maintenance
Scalability
Types of Network Topologies

There are six major topologies:

Bus Topology
Star Topology
Ring Topology
Mesh Topology
Tree Topology
Hybrid Topology
1. Bus Topology
Definition

In a Bus Topology, all devices share one common cable, called the backbone cable.

Diagram
Computer ----+
             |
Laptop ------+==================== Backbone Cable
             |
Printer -----+
             |
Server ------+

Every device is connected to the same main cable.

How Does It Work?

Suppose Computer A wants to send data to Printer.

Computer A sends the data onto the backbone cable.
The data travels along the cable.
Every connected device "sees" the data.
Only the printer accepts it because the data contains the printer's address.
Other devices ignore it.
Real-Life Example

Imagine a classroom where everyone shares one microphone.

When one student speaks:

Everyone hears the sound.
Only the intended person responds.

The microphone is like the backbone cable.

Advantages
Simple design.
Low installation cost.
Requires less cable.
Easy to set up for small networks.
Disadvantages
If the backbone cable fails, the entire network stops working.
Performance decreases as more devices are added.
Difficult to troubleshoot.
Data collisions are more likely.
Cybersecurity Perspective

If an attacker gains access to the shared medium, they may be able to capture traffic from multiple devices. Modern switched networks reduce this risk.

2. Star Topology
Definition

In a Star Topology, every device connects to a central device, usually a switch.

Diagram
             PC 1
               |
               |
PC 2 ------ Switch ------ PC 3
               |
               |
             Server
               |
            Printer

No device connects directly to another; all communication passes through the switch.

How Does It Work?

Suppose PC 1 wants to send data to the Printer.

PC 1 sends the data to the switch.
The switch checks the destination.
The switch forwards the data only to the printer.

Unlike a hub, a switch doesn't broadcast to every device.

Real-Life Example

Think of an airport.

Passengers don't fly directly between every pair of cities. They often go through a central airport (hub) before reaching their destination.

The switch acts like that central hub.

Advantages
Easy to install.
Easy to troubleshoot.
One cable failure affects only one device.
High performance with switches.
Easy to add new devices.
Disadvantages
If the central switch fails, the whole network is affected.
More cable is required.
Slightly higher cost.
Cybersecurity Perspective

Most modern organizations use Star Topology because switches support features like:

VLANs
Port security
MAC address filtering
Network monitoring

These improve both security and performance.

3. Ring Topology
Definition

In a Ring Topology, each device is connected to exactly two neighboring devices, forming a closed loop.

Diagram
        PC1 -------- PC2
         |             |
         |             |
        PC4 -------- PC3
How Does It Work?

If PC1 sends data to PC3:

PC1 → PC2 → PC3

The data travels around the ring until it reaches the destination.

Some ring networks use a token, a special control message that grants permission to transmit. Only the device holding the token can send data, which helps avoid collisions.

Advantages
Predictable performance.
Fewer collisions (especially with token-based systems).
Fair access to the network.
Disadvantages
One broken connection can disrupt the entire ring (unless redundancy is built in).
Harder to expand.
More difficult to troubleshoot than a star.
Real-Life Example

Imagine people standing in a circle passing a parcel to the next person until it reaches the intended recipient.

4. Mesh Topology
Definition

In a Mesh Topology, devices have multiple paths to communicate with one another.

There are two types:

Full Mesh

Every device connects directly to every other device.

A -------- B
|\        /|
| \      / |
|  \    /  |
|   \  /   |
|    \/    |
|    /\    |
|   /  \   |
|  /    \  |
| /      \ |
|/        \|
C -------- D
Partial Mesh

Only some devices have multiple direct connections.

How Does It Work?

If one connection fails, the data can take another route.

Example:

A → B → D
If B fails:
A → C → D
Advantages
Very reliable.
High availability.
No single communication path.
Excellent fault tolerance.
Disadvantages
Expensive.
Requires many cables and ports.
Complex installation and maintenance.
Real-Life Example

The Internet is not one giant full mesh, but many parts of it use mesh-like connectivity so traffic can be rerouted around failures.

Cybersecurity Perspective

Mesh networks improve resilience. Even if one link is attacked or fails, communication can often continue through alternate paths.

5. Tree Topology
Definition

A Tree Topology combines characteristics of Star and Bus topologies.

Diagram
             Core Switch
                 |
      -----------------------
      |                     |
 Department A         Department B
      |                     |
   --------             --------
   |      |             |      |
 PC1    PC2          PC3     PC4
Real-Life Example

A university network:

Core switch in the data center.
Separate switches for each building.
End-user devices connected to those switches.

This hierarchical design scales well.

Advantages
Highly scalable.
Easier to organize large networks.
Simple to manage by sections.
Disadvantages
If the core device fails, large parts of the network may become unavailable.
More complex than a simple star.
Cybersecurity Perspective

Tree topologies allow network segmentation, making it easier to isolate departments and apply different security policies.

6. Hybrid Topology
Definition

A Hybrid Topology combines two or more different topologies.

Example

A company may use:

Star topology inside each office.
Mesh links between data centers.
Tree structure for campus distribution.
Advantages
Flexible.
Scalable.
Can be optimized for different environments.
Disadvantages
Complex design.
Higher cost.
Requires careful planning.
Real-Life Example

Large enterprises, cloud providers, and multinational companies almost always use hybrid topologies rather than relying on a single design.

Comparison Table
Topology	Cost	Reliability	Easy to Expand	Single Point of Failure
Bus	Low	Low	No	Backbone cable
Star	Medium	High	Yes	Central switch
Ring	Medium	Medium	Difficult	Ring break (without redundancy)
Mesh	High	Very High	Moderate	Very few
Tree	Medium-High	High	Yes	Core device
Hybrid	High	Very High	Excellent	Depends on design
Which Topology Is Used Today?

Modern networks commonly use:

Homes: Star
Schools and Colleges: Star + Tree
Corporate Offices: Star
Data Centers: Leaf-spine architectures (a specialized form of tree/mesh concepts)
Internet Backbone: Highly redundant mesh-like connectivity

Pure bus and ring topologies are now uncommon in new Ethernet deployments.
